# Target C - terminal FREE branch forces a sharp chamber-interface floor

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only synthesis; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II survivor sequences carrying the current terminal FREE branch, with `pi D-q=o(D)`  
**Depends on:** canonical Sections 63, 67, and 85; audited-working all-defect stopped-train/phase gap Section 142; the current terminal FREE reduction only for the branch hypothesis  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_chamber_interface_floor_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The latest terminal FREE notes force a first-order strict-rhombus count deficit. This note extracts a different first-order consequence from the same terminal geometry: the raw inactive-chamber interface itself must be very large, and one of its two geometric classes must already be linearly large.

Put

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},\qquad c_0={\sqrt3\over4}={a_0\over2},
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\]

and

\[
\gamma:=2s_2-{\pi\over2}.
\]

Let

\[
U:=q-k,\qquad t_D:={U\over D},\qquad p:=\operatorname{per}(F),
\qquad \lambda_D:=b-p\ge0.
\]

For the bounded nonquadrilateral faces, let `f` be their number and
`Q=sum_P(deg P-4)`. Since every such face has degree at least five,

\[
Q\ge f.
\tag{CF.1}
\]

Define the canonical Section-85 raw chamber-interface currency

\[
\mathcal H_D:=(b-p)+(Q+2f)=\lambda_D+Q+2f.
\tag{CF.2}
\]

The main result is

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\mathcal H_D\over D}
\ge {12\over5}s_2
=2.285550736473497\ldots .
}
\tag{CF.3}
\]

Consequently Section 85 gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge {6a_0\over5}s_2
=0.989672499712141\ldots .
}
\tag{CF.4}
\]

This is terminal-specific. It does not replace the older general full-core `0.586223...D` chamber-surplus floor outside the terminal branch.

There is also a classwise strengthening. Write

- `A_exc,Z_def` for the total area and assigned isolate population in bounded nonquadrilateral faces;
- `H_out,Z_in` for the total area and assigned isolate population in the convex outer pockets of Section 67.

Then one of the two chamber classes alone satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\max\{
 A_{exc}-a_0Z_{def},
 H_{out}-a_0Z_{in}
\}
\ge
\left({3a_0s_2\over4}-o(1)\right)D,
}
\tag{CF.5}
\]

where

\[
{3a_0s_2\over4}
=0.618545312320088\ldots .
\tag{CF.6}
\]

Equivalently, before applying Oler to the two classes,

\[
\boxed{
\max\{Q+2f,\lambda_D\}
\ge
\left({3s_2\over2}-o(1)\right)D
= (1.428469210295936\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{CF.7}
\]

The constants in (CF.3) and (CF.7) are sharp for the relaxed scalar system used here. Improving either requires new geometry, not another rearrangement of the same terminal, face-degree, and all-defect inequalities.

---

## 1. Exact terminal interface identity

Canonical Section 63 gives

\[
\boxed{b+Q=2(q-k)=2U.}
\tag{CF.8}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{Q+\lambda_D=Q+b-p=2U-p.}
\tag{CF.9}
\]

On the terminal branch

\[
\pi D-q=o(D).
\tag{CF.10}
\]

Every force-hull side joins distinct source points and has length at least one, so

\[
q\le p\le\pi D.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{{p\over D}\to\pi.}
\tag{CF.11}
\]

The audited-working all-defect Section-142 theorem gives

\[
\boxed{2f-k\ge(\gamma-o(1))D.}
\tag{CF.12}
\]

Since `k=q-U`, terminality gives

\[
{k\over D}=\pi-t_D+o(1).
\tag{CF.13}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
{f\over D}
\ge {\pi-t_D+\gamma\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{CF.14}
\]

No FREE-specific matching or reflection count is used in (CF.8)-(CF.14). The FREE hypothesis enters only because the current live reduction places the branch under study on the terminal face.

---

## 2. Terminal feasibility already forces `t_D>=1.951721...`

Equation (CF.9), nonnegativity of `lambda_D`, and (CF.1) imply

\[
2U-p=Q+\lambda_D\ge f.
\]

Divide by `D` and use (CF.11):

\[
2t_D-\pi\ge {f\over D}-o(1).
\tag{CF.15}
\]

Insert (CF.14):

\[
2t_D-\pi
\ge {\pi-t_D+\gamma\over2}-o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 t_D\ge t_0-o(1),
 \qquad
 t_0:={3\pi+\gamma\over5}
 ={\pi\over2}+{2s_2\over5}
 =1.951721449540479\ldots .
}
\tag{CF.16}
\]

The numerical value has appeared elsewhere in the research tail under stronger or different route hypotheses. The point here is the short terminal derivation: on `q/D->pi`, the all-defect face floor and the elementary feasibility `Q+lambda>=f` already force it.

---

## 3. The raw Section-85 interface is at least `2.285550...D`

From (CF.2) and (CF.9),

\[
\mathcal H_D
=2U-p+2f.
\tag{CF.17}
\]

Divide by `D`, use (CF.11), and then (CF.14):

\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathcal H_D\over D}
&=2t_D-\pi+2{f\over D}+o(1)\\
&\ge 2t_D-\pi+(\pi-t_D+\gamma)-o(1)\\
&=t_D+\gamma-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{CF.18}
\]

Insert (CF.16):

\[
\liminf {\mathcal H_D\over D}
\ge t_0+\gamma.
\]

The constants simplify exactly:

\[
t_0+\gamma
=\left({\pi\over2}+{2s_2\over5}\right)
 +\left(2s_2-{\pi\over2}\right)
={12s_2\over5}.
\]

This proves (CF.3).

### Sharpness of the current scalar relaxation

Equality in the limiting scalar system is possible at

\[
t=t_0,\qquad
{f\over D}={Q\over D}={4s_2\over5},
\qquad
{\lambda_D\over D}=0.
\tag{CF.19}
\]

Indeed, (CF.14) and (CF.15) are then equalities. Moreover the current FREE recipient laws can also be saturated at the same scalar point by taking

\[
{Z_{ext}\over D}={1\over2}.
\]

Then `lambda_D+Z_ext=D/2` and

\[
t+{2\over5}{Z_{ext}\over D}
=t_0+{1\over5}
={\pi\over2}+{1\over5}+{2s_2\over5},
\]

which is exactly the current terminal FREE train/face constant. Thus (CF.3) cannot be improved by recombining only these scalar constraints.

---

## 4. Convert the interface floor to actual inactive-chamber surplus

Canonical Section 67 gives the two chamber-class inequalities separately.

For bounded nonquadrilateral faces,

\[
\boxed{
A_{exc}-a_0Z_{def}
\ge a_0f+c_0Q
=c_0(2f+Q).
}
\tag{CF.20}
\]

For the outer pockets,

\[
\boxed{
H_{out}-a_0Z_{in}
\ge c_0(b-p)
=c_0\lambda_D.
}
\tag{CF.21}
\]

Adding them gives the canonical Section-85 inequality in its exact class decomposition:

\[
\boxed{
A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}
\ge c_0\mathcal H_D.
}
\tag{CF.22}
\]

Equation (CF.3) therefore gives

\[
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge c_0{12s_2\over5}
={6a_0s_2\over5},
\]

which is (CF.4).

This is stronger than the older proof-only `0.586223D` floor on the present terminal branch, but it does not contradict Type II: a linear Oler surplus can still be spread across `O(D)` thin chambers.

---

## 5. One chamber class alone carries `0.618545...D`

The total floor does not by itself identify where the interface is paid. A sharper minimax does.

Normalize

\[
F_D:={f\over D},\qquad
Q_D:={Q\over D},\qquad
L_D:={\lambda_D\over D}.
\]

The raw exceptional class has normalized size

\[
E_D:=2F_D+Q_D\ge3F_D
\tag{CF.23}
\]

because `Q_D>=F_D`.

Also, by (CF.17) and (CF.14),

\[
E_D+L_D
={\mathcal H_D\over D}
\ge 4s_2-2F_D-o(1).
\tag{CF.24}
\]

To see the last expression directly, rewrite (CF.14) as

\[
t_D\ge\pi+\gamma-2F_D-o(1)
\]

and insert it into

\[
{\mathcal H_D\over D}=2t_D-\pi+2F_D+o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
\max\{E_D,L_D\}
\ge
\max\left\{3F_D,\,2s_2-F_D\right\}-o(1).
\tag{CF.25}
\]

The right side is minimized when

\[
3F_D=2s_2-F_D,
\qquad F_D={s_2\over2},
\]

and the minimum is

\[
{3s_2\over2}.
\]

This proves (CF.7). Multiplying by `c_0` and using (CF.20)-(CF.21) proves (CF.5).

The split constant is also sharp for the relaxed scalar system. Take

\[
{f\over D}={Q\over D}={s_2\over2},
\qquad
 t={\pi\over2}+s_2,
\qquad
 {\lambda_D\over D}={3s_2\over2}.
\tag{CF.26}
\]

Then the all-defect inequality is tight and

\[
2{f\over D}+{Q\over D}
={\lambda_D\over D}
={3s_2\over2}.
\]

Hence no larger classwise constant follows from the scalar constraints used here.

---

## 6. Relation to the new rhombus-deficit floor

The immediately preceding terminal FREE theorem proves

\[
\mathcal D_4:=\Delta_2D^2-R_4
\ge(1.2427753682\ldots-o(1))D+{4\over5}Z_{ext}.
\]

The present theorem is complementary rather than a rearrangement of that count deficit. It says that the inactive-chamber part of the force-hull decomposition must itself carry

\[
A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}
\ge(0.9896724997\ldots-o(1))D,
\]

and that at least one of the bounded-exceptional or outer-pocket classes carries at least

\[
(0.6185453123\ldots-o(1))D
\]

of this Oler-weighted surplus.

Using the exact geometric identity

\[
a_0\mathcal D_4=\delta_F+A_{ch}+E_4,
\]

the rhombus-count deficit and the chamber-interface theorem now locate two different first-order costs in the same terminal survivor: a global strict-rhombus deficiency and a large source-level chamber/interface payment.

---

## 7. Remaining bottleneck

This still does not close C-II. The missing implication is now more localized.

A terminal FREE survivor must satisfy both

\[
\mathcal D_4\ge(1.2427753682\ldots-o(1))D+{4\over5}Z_{ext}
\]

and

\[
\mathcal H_D\ge(2.2855507364\ldots-o(1))D,
\]

with one of `Q+2f` or `b-per(F)` at least

\[
(1.4284692102\ldots-o(1))D.
\]

The next useful theorem should attack these two class branches separately:

1. **Exceptional-face branch:** transfer a linear `Q+2f` population into the occurrence-counted Section-122/123 attachment-cell detour, or prove that the accompanying first-order rhombus deficit cannot be supported by the full-core harmonic tiling.
2. **Outer-pocket branch:** convert a linear `b-per(F)` into a source-preserving strict diameter decrease, or into an upper budget incompatible with the terminal near-disk force hull.

A generic statement that `A_ch-a_0Z_ch=Omega(D)` is no longer enough. The classwise split (CF.7) is the sharper handoff.

**Disposition:** proved a sharp terminal raw-interface floor, the resulting `0.989672...D` chamber-surplus floor, and a `0.618545...D` one-class surplus dichotomy. The authoritative theorem-state ledger remains unchanged; C-II remains open.
